 ## Overview
-The SNS backward pass computes gradients for the 3D Skew-Normal Splatting (SNS) parameters by decoupling the complex interaction between rotation and scale matrices through an intermediate variable $Q$. Unlike standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) which computes gradients with respect to the inverse covariance matrix $\Omega^{-1}$ before backpropagating to rotation $R$ and scale $S$, SNS introduces $Q = ARS$ to reformulate the 2D Skew-Normal density function [S1, unofficial]. This approach avoids the high computational complexity of directly differentiating the coupled $R$ and $S$ parameters, instead evaluating shared auxiliary algebraic terms during the rendering stage and using closed-form expressions in the preprocessing stage to compute exact gradients for 3D learnable parameters [S1, unofficial].
+The SNS Backward Pass with Intermediate Variable Q computes the gradients of the loss with respect to the 3D Gaussian parameters (mean, skew, rotation, scale, color, opacity) for Skew-Normal Splatting [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of high computational complexity and intricate coupling between rotation $R$ and scale $S$ in standard 3DGS gradient computation by introducing an intermediate variable $Q = ARS$ [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard 3DGS, which computes gradients w.r.t. 2D image-space variables first, SNS directly evaluates shared auxiliary algebraic terms during the rendering stage to enable efficient closed-form gradient evaluation for 3D parameters in the preprocessing stage [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The backward pass follows the standard 3DGS architecture, divided into a rendering stage and a preprocessing stage [S1, unofficial]. The rendering stage computes gradients with respect to 2D image-space variables and evaluates shared auxiliary terms. The preprocessing stage uses these terms to compute gradients for 3D parameters [S1, unofficial].
+**Inputs and Outputs:**
+*   **Inputs:** Loss gradient w.r.t. rendered color $\frac{\partial L}{\partial C}$, rendered color $C$, opacity $a$, 2D Skew-Normal density $SN^{2D}$, pixel coordinate $u$, 2D mean $\mu^{2D}$, intermediate variable $Q$, skew parameter $k$, 2D covariance matrix $\Omega^{2D}$, and the Gaussian component $G_2$.
+*   **Outputs:** Gradients $\frac{\partial L}{\partial c}$, $\frac{\partial L}{\partial o}$, $\frac{\partial L}{\partial \mu}$, $\frac{\partial L}{\partial SN^{2D}}$, $\frac{\partial L}{\partial k}$, and $\frac{\partial L}{\partial Q}$ [S1, unofficial].
 
